# Do not regress existing features

At the start of a task, name the files you will change and the areas below that own them. Before you finish, every feature in those areas must still behave as written here. A feature on another branch counts: if your checkout is missing it and you are editing its files, bring that behavior forward unless the user told you to remove it. Switching to `main` is not a reason to replace a newer behavior with the older one.

When you add, change, or remove a feature, update this rule in the same change: area, files, the behavior that must keep working, and the branch if it is not on `main`.

Check the area you touched, not the whole app. Backend areas: run `uv run pytest` from `backend/`. UI areas: exercise the interaction.

## Conversation list

Files: `frontend/src/App.tsx`, `frontend/src/api.ts`, conversation routes in `backend/app/main.py`, conversation helpers in `backend/app/db.py`.

- The header control lists chats newest first. Choosing a row opens that chat.
- Each row has Delete. Confirm with the goal snippet, or "New conversation". Deleting the open chat opens the next remaining chat; deleting a different chat leaves you where you are. The last delete shows the empty state.
- `DELETE /api/conversations/{id}` returns 204, or 404. Threads and messages point at each other, so null `fork_message_id`, `parent_thread_id`, and `parent_id` before deleting messages, threads, then the conversation. The conversation's chunks_fts, chunks, and documents rows are deleted before the conversation row.
- New conversation creates an empty center node titled "Center".

## Turns, fork, and context

Files: `backend/app/context.py`, `backend/app/db.py`, chat routes in `backend/app/main.py`, `frontend/src/ChatPane.tsx`, `frontend/src/Message.tsx`, `frontend/src/SelectionPopover.tsx`.

- Context for a turn is only the `parent_id` walk to the root. The center node never receives side-node tokens. A side node does not see messages added to its parent after the fork. Sibling forks do not see each other.
- A side node can be forked only from an assistant message. Its first message's `parent_id` is that fork message.
- A failed turn deletes the user message it just saved and does not keep a goal or title from that turn.
- Stored message content is raw Anthropic blocks and is not edited. Messages are deleted only by the failed-turn rollback, by deleting a side node (its subtree), and by deleting a whole conversation.
- Every assistant message has Fork. A text selection offers Fork from this (quote in the composer) and Explain this (quote plus "Explain this." sent immediately).
- Fork stays on screen while a long reply scrolls (`sticky bottom-3` on the Fork button in `Message.tsx`). Introduced on `sticky-fork-button`, now on `main`. `frontend/src/Message.test.tsx` covers it: the button keeps `sticky` and `bottom-3`, its nearest non-`contents` ancestor is the `<article>`, and it is never inside a `<p>`. Keep the `pointer-events-none contents` wrapper.

## Tree and node titles

Files: `backend/app/llm.py` (`suggest_title`), `note_user_message` in `backend/app/db.py`, `_name_first_turn` in `backend/app/main.py`, `frontend/src/TreeView.tsx`, `frontend/src/Breadcrumb.tsx`.

- After a node's first reply succeeds, its title is a 2–6 word topic name from Claude Haiku 4.5, using the first 400 characters of that node's first user message and the first 400 characters of the reply text. The title must agree with the reply and must not state a fact the reply contradicts. A name chosen from the user message alone is a regression once a reply can contain facts the title model does not know, which a web search does. A failed, empty, or cut-off title call uses the first 40 characters of the user message. A reply with no text gets that same 40-character fallback, and the title model is not called. Later messages do not rename the node. Introduced on `haiku-node-titles`.
- Tree cards are as wide as the title and the "Center" / "Side node" label, so the name is not clipped. Hover shows the fork snippet. Click switches node. Children are ordered by fork position, then creation time. The open node is highlighted.
- The center node stays on the tree after a fork. Cards are remeasured when the graph changes, so an existing node is not left hidden when its size did not change.
- Side-node cards show Delete on hover; the center card does not. Clicking Delete does not switch nodes. `frontend/src/TreeView.test.tsx` covers both.
- A side node shows the breadcrumb from center. Clicking a breadcrumb title or a tree card switches to that node. There is no Back to center button.

## Sidebar width

Files: `frontend/src/sidebar.ts`, `frontend/src/ResizeHandle.tsx`, the `<aside>` in `frontend/src/App.tsx`, the pane-width `fitView` effect in `frontend/src/TreeView.tsx`, `frontend/src/sidebar.test.ts`, `frontend/src/ResizeHandle.test.tsx`.

- The sidebar (Documents and Tree) is resized by dragging its left edge. Dragging left widens it; the chat pane takes the rest because it is `flex-1 min-w-0`.
- Width defaults to 384px, never goes below 280px, and never leaves the chat under 360px. It is stored in `localStorage` (`tangents-sidebar-width`). A window resize re-clamps from the stored value without overwriting it.
- Dragging writes the width to the DOM on each move and commits state plus storage once on pointer up, so long chats do not re-render during a drag. While dragging, body text selection is off so the drag never opens the selection popover.
- The handle is a focusable `role="separator"`: ArrowLeft/ArrowRight change it by 16px (64px with Shift). Double-click resets to 384px. It works while a reply streams.
- The tree re-fits (`padding 0.25`, `maxZoom 1`) whenever its pane width changes.
- Introduced on `resizable-tree`.

## Pinned goal

Files: `frontend/src/PinnedGoal.tsx`, `frontend/src/PinnedGoal.test.tsx`, the `<section>` in `frontend/src/ChatPane.tsx`, `update_goal` in `backend/app/db.py`, `PATCH /api/conversations/{id}`.

- The goal starts as the center node's first user message, and only while `goal` is still null. Editing it does not change the transcript. Later messages do not overwrite an edited goal.
- It stays at the top of every node, truncated to two lines, expandable. Enter or blur saves; Esc cancels.
- The collapsed goal is clamped by `line-clamp-2` alone. Do not add a display class (`block`, `flex`, ...) next to it: that overrides the `-webkit-box` display the clamp needs, and a long first prompt then fills the whole pane. Expanded, it is capped at `max-h-48` and scrolls, keeping line breaks. The chat `<section>` is `min-h-0 overflow-hidden`, so nothing above the message list can push the composer off screen. `frontend/src/PinnedGoal.test.tsx` covers the classes.

## Streaming, cache, and compaction

Files: `backend/app/llm.py`, `backend/app/config.py`, `frontend/src/CompactionDivider.tsx`, `frontend/src/UsageFooter.tsx`, `frontend/src/ThinkingSection.tsx`, `frontend/src/Message.tsx`.

- A chat turn streams `user_message`, `thinking`, `delta`, `compaction`, `web_activity`, `web_result`, `done`, and `error`. The footer shows `in`, `out`, `cache read`, and `cache write`.
- Cache breakpoints are added on a deep copy. Stored rows are not mutated. At most two breakpoints: the last block of the inherited path that is not a thinking block, and the last block of the new user message that is not a thinking block. A message made only of thinking blocks gets no breakpoint.
- `max_tokens` is the model's maximum (128000, or 64000 for Haiku 4.5). `MAX_TOKENS` is an optional ceiling, used only when it is lower.
- A `pause_turn` continues within the same turn, for at most 5 continuations, and still saves one assistant message.
- `stop_reason` and `thinking_ms` are stored on `usage`. A `max_tokens` stop shows "Claude hit the maximum length for this message." and a Continue button.
- Changing effort or model mid-conversation costs one cache miss on the next turn. That is expected.
- `stream_chat` joins the web and docs system instructions with a blank line when both apply. Either one alone is sent by itself. Neither means no system prompt.
- Compaction uses `compact-2026-01-12` when the chosen model supports it. The summary must restate the pinned goal. The chat shows Compacted here above that message; expanding it shows the summary. Older messages stay. There is no manual compact button.
- `CACHE_TTL` is `5m` or `1h`. `COMPACT_TRIGGER_TOKENS` defaults to 150000 and refuses values under 50000. The API key stays on the server.

## Web search and fetch

Files: `web_tools` / `history_has_web_blocks` / `_attach_web_tools` in `backend/app/llm.py`, the web settings in `backend/app/config.py`, chat routes and `slim_message` in `backend/app/main.py`, `frontend/src/websearch.ts`, `frontend/src/WebActivity.tsx`, `frontend/src/Message.tsx`, the header in `frontend/src/App.tsx`.

- The header has Web and Docs toggles. Each is per turn, defaults to off, and is remembered in `localStorage` (`tangents-web-search`, `tangents-doc-search`). Both sit outside `menuRef` so the conversation dropdown's outside-click and Escape handlers still work. Docs is disabled while the conversation has no documents. The model menu is next to Send and has its own outside-click handling.
- Search is never forced. With the toggle on, `tool_choice` stays unset and a system instruction tells Claude to look up facts that can change, recent events, specific pages, and anything it is not sure is still true, and not to answer those from memory. A turn that does not search is still correct when the conversation itself is enough. The instruction is omitted when the toggle is off.
- Both tools are Anthropic server tools (`web_search_20260318`, `web_fetch_20260318`), so a turn that searches is still one request and one saved assistant message. No `tool_use` or `tool_result` rows ever enter the tree. Do not replace these with a client-side search library without redesigning how tool rows live in a forkable tree. Both tools set `allowed_callers` to `["direct"]`. The SDK default includes code-execution callers, and Haiku 4.5 rejects that.
- `web_fetch` may only fetch URLs the user pasted or that `web_search` returned: `url_sources` sets `user_input` to `all`, `server_tool_results` to `only` naming `web_search`, and `client_tool_results` to `none`. Keep that policy.
- Stored web blocks are replayed on every later turn, so `_attach_web_tools` declares the tools whenever `history_has_web_blocks(path)` is true even if the toggle is off, and then sets `tool_choice` to `{"type": "none"}`. Without this, toggling search off mid-thread and forking from a searched reply both break.
- `encrypted_content`, fetched page text, thinking `signature`, and redacted thinking `data` are stripped from HTTP responses by `slim_message`, applied only to the `done` event and `GET /api/threads/{id}/messages`. Stored rows keep the full blocks, because the API requires encrypted content back unmodified. `slim_message` must never mutate its input.
- A finished assistant message with web blocks shows a collapsible "Searched the web" row listing source links. During the stream, `web_activity` and `web_result` events show what is being searched or read. The row is `select-none` so selecting a source title does not trigger the fork popover.
- The usage footer adds `searches` and `fetches` from `usage.server_tool_use`, shown only when non-zero. The four existing counters keep their order and wording.
- Tool-level failures (`too_many_requests`, `url_not_allowed`, `max_uses_exceeded`, `url_not_in_prior_context`) come back as error blocks inside a result block, so the turn still succeeds and saves. Only a hard API error rolls the turn back.
- `WEB_SEARCH_MAX_USES` and `WEB_FETCH_MAX_USES` default to 5 and refuse values under 1. `WEB_FETCH_MAX_CONTENT_TOKENS` defaults to 20000. `WEB_SEARCH_BLOCKED_DOMAINS` is an optional comma-separated list. The API key still stays on the server; the browser sends only a boolean.
- Cache note: tool definitions sit ahead of the messages in the cached prefix, so flipping the toggle mid-conversation costs one cache miss on the next turn. That is expected, not a bug.
- A searched reply can establish a fact the title model does not know. The node title is chosen in the same turn as that reply, and it must agree with the reply. See Tree and node titles. Leaving the title call on the user message alone will name the node from memory and contradict the answer.
- Introduced on `main`.

## Model and theme

Files: `frontend/src/models.ts`, `frontend/src/ModelMenu.tsx`, `frontend/src/Composer.tsx`, `frontend/src/ModelMenu.test.tsx`, `frontend/src/theme.ts`, the header in `frontend/src/App.tsx`.

- The model menu sits next to Send. Choices: Sonnet 5.5, Opus 5.5, Fable 5.1, and Haiku 4.5. Effort runs from Low to Max on the three 5.x models, with defaults of high, medium, and high. Haiku has no effort setting and has an Extended thinking toggle with a budget of 16000. Model, effort per model, and extended thinking are stored in `localStorage` (`tangents-model`, `tangents-effort`, `tangents-extended-thinking`). The menu has its own outside-click handling, separate from `menuRef`.
- Dark mode follows the system until toggled; the choice is stored in `localStorage`.

## Node delete

Files: `delete_thread` in `backend/app/db.py`, `DELETE /api/threads/{id}` in `backend/app/main.py`, `deleteThread` in `frontend/src/api.ts`, `removeThread` in `frontend/src/App.tsx`, the Delete control in `frontend/src/TreeView.tsx`.

- Deleting a side node deletes it and every descendant node, with their messages. Children cannot outlive their parent: their fork message and context path live in it.
- The center node cannot be deleted (400). Deleting the conversation is how you remove it. Unknown id is 404; success is 204.
- Clear `fork_message_id`, `parent_thread_id`, and `parent_id` inside the subtree before deleting messages, then threads. Nothing outside the subtree changes; the parent's fork message stays.
- Confirm names the node and how many side nodes go with it. If the open node was removed, open the deleted node's parent; otherwise stay where you are.
- Delete is disabled while a reply streams. A turn whose node is deleted mid-stream rolls back through the normal error path.

## Document retrieval

Files: `backend/app/rag.py`, `backend/app/pdf.py`, the `documents` / `chunks` / `chunks_fts` schema and `delete_conversation` in `backend/app/db.py`, `EMBED_MODEL`, `RAG_TOP_K`, `MAX_UPLOAD_MB`, and `PDF_MAX_PAGES` in `backend/app/config.py`, document routes in `backend/app/main.py`, `frontend/src/DocumentsPanel.tsx`, `frontend/src/DocPassages.tsx`, `frontend/src/docsearch.ts`, the header and sidebar in `frontend/src/App.tsx`, `backend/tests/test_rag.py`, `backend/tests/test_pdf.py`, `backend/scripts/bench_pdf.py`, `backend/bench/`.

- Documents belong to one conversation. `search` only returns chunks whose `conversation_id` matches. Every node in that conversation can search them.
- `.txt`, `.md`, `.markdown` are decoded as UTF-8. `.docx`, `.pptx`, `.xlsx`, `.html`, `.htm`, `.epub` go through markitdown. `.pdf` is read locally, page by page: the text layer when a page has one, RapidOCR when it does not. RapidLayout (`pp_doc_layoutv3`) marks titles and tables and drops headers and footers. Text-layer tables come from pdfplumber; scanned tables come from RapidTable. A table continued on the next page is merged into one table. `force_ocr` is only for the benchmark, not for upload. Anything else, or a file with no text, raises `UnsupportedDocument`. A PDF over `PDF_MAX_PAGES` (default 200, refuses values under 1) raises `UnsupportedDocument`.
- Chunks split on Markdown headings first, then a word window (default 180 words, 30 overlap). Each chunk keeps its heading path joined with " > ". Pipe tables keep line breaks, split by whole rows, repeat the header, and never mix with prose. A `<!-- table-caption: ... -->` line is appended to that table chunk's heading.
- Embeddings are local (`fastembed`, default `snowflake/snowflake-arctic-embed-m`), normalized float32, cached under `backend/.cache/`. Changing `EMBED_MODEL` requires re-ingesting.
- Queries get the model's retrieval prefix from `QUERY_PREFIXES`; passages get none. A model not in the map gets no prefix.
- `search` modes are `hybrid` (default), `vector`, `keyword`. Hybrid merges vector cosine and FTS5 bm25 with reciprocal rank fusion (k=60). User text reaches FTS5 only as quoted words joined by OR.
- The sidebar Documents panel lists a conversation's files, uploads them, and deletes one at a time. `POST /api/conversations/{id}/documents` returns 201, 404 for an unknown conversation, 400 for an unsupported file, and 413 over `MAX_UPLOAD_MB` (default 25, refuses values under 1). `GET` returns the list or 404. `DELETE /api/documents/{id}` returns 204, or 404.
- A Docs toggle, off by default, asks a turn to search. With it on and at least one document, the saved user message is `search_result` blocks (citations enabled) followed by the typed text. Those blocks are never `tool_use` / `tool_result` rows. Forks inherit them because they are on the parent path. The goal and the node title still come from the typed text only. With the toggle off, or with no documents, the user row is text only.
- A user message with those blocks shows a collapsible "Searched your documents" row. It is `select-none`.
- Tests never download the embedding model; they use a fake embedder. PDF tests use fake layout, OCR, and table engines and never download those models. `backend/scripts/bench_pdf.py` is not a pytest test; it uses the real models.
- Introduced on `pdf-layout-ocr`.

## Removed

- Merge back is gone. There is no summary call, no merge route, and no control that copies a side node onto its parent. A side node's messages stay on that node. A stored row with `is_note` still renders as a note; nothing creates new notes.
- Back to center is gone. The tree and the breadcrumb are how you leave a side node.
